{"id":"the-complete-alice-in-wonderland-en_sct116_f1","schemaVersion":1,"fragmentId":"sha1:a3007bb60b1ce028b4154f0c73dcf7dbf803389e","generationHash":"sha1:2ce838f72dd8f7e4a44313aa3b8aeacb4b620d53","title":"The Complete Alice in Wonderland - The Old Man’s Comforts and How He Gained Them (Fragment 1)","text":"The Old Man’s Comforts and How He Gained Them\n\nby Robert Southey\n\n“You are old, father William,” the young man cried,\n\n“The few locks which are left you are grey;\n\nYou are hale, father William, a hearty old man;\n\nNow tell me the reason, I pray.”\n\n“In the days of my youth,” father William replied,\n\n“I remember’d that youth would fly fast,\n\nAnd abus’d not my health and my vigour at first,\n\nThat I never might need them at last.”\n\n“You are old, father William,” the young man cried,\n\n“And pleasures with youth pass away.\n\nAnd yet you lament not the days that are gone;\n\nNow tell me the reason I pray.”\n\n“In the days of my youth,” father William replied,\n\n“I remember’d that youth could not last;\n\nI thought of the future, whatever I did,\n\nThat I never might grieve for the past.”\n\n“You are old, father William,” the young man cried,\n\n“And life must be hast’ning away;\n\nYou are cheerful and love to converse upon death;\n\nNow tell me the reason, I pray.”\n\n“I am cheerful, young man,” father William replied,\n\n“Let the cause thy attention engage;\n\nIn the days of my youth I remember’d my God!\n\nAnd He hath not forgotten my age.”","meta":{"bookId":"the-complete-alice-in-wonderland-en","bookTitle":"The Complete Alice in Wonderland","bookLanguage":"en","author":"Lewis Carroll","seriesId":"alice","seriesTitle":"Alice","sectionIndex":116,"sectionTitle":"The Old Man’s Comforts and How He Gained Them","fragmentIndex":1,"fragmentsInSection":1,"sourceFile":"chapter_116.xhtml","stats":{"wordCount":206,"uniqueWordCount":104,"paragraphCount":26,"vocabularyCount":50,"levelDistribution":{"A1":17,"A2":12,"B1":12,"B2":9,"C1":0,"C2":0}}},"vocabulary"